00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:20.070
you're doing really well in your career.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.740
But at this point you choose to
make two pretty big decisions.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.330
One, you leave banking to
start your own business.

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.770
And two,
you decided to move back to China.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.850
Tell us more about your
thoughts behind that decision.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.520
&gt;&gt; I think I can talk a little
bit sort of the thoughts

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:44.400
applied to my decision to go
back to China star sea trip.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.170
I think as someone who left China
after four years of college,

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.630
he always want to do some scene for
China or around China.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:01.200
Obviously upon graduation from Chang Tong,
probably the best choice for

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.260
me at that time is go to U.S. and study.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.160
But, while I was in New York I
always thinking, can I do something?

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.480
Can I leverage my China experience,
or background?

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:18.025
So, 1992, 1994, there's an opportunity

00:02:18.025 --> 00:02:21.985
you can go back to China and be part of
the so called capital markets efforts.

00:02:21.985 --> 00:02:28.215
Many students who study in the US or

00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:33.685
work in the U.S. before, end up joining
those Asian branch of big investment bank.

00:02:33.685 --> 00:02:34.975
And I'm part of them.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:41.600
And you get very excited, because when
you back you see how China has changed

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.600
from the 1980s against
the time when I left China.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:51.770
So you really feel like there's
a lot of things you can do.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:57.750
But back in 1994, 1995 most of the so

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:04.010
called economic reform around big
enterprises, especially SOE's.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:10.320
What really actually have changed
is toward the end of 1990,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.550
you know the 1990's,
when private enterprises

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:18.790
come to become one of the major force
in the whole economic development.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:26.840
And so people like myself when
I first working in it you know?

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.640
In the events and banking world in China,
my major customers are you know,

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.040
the Chinese corporates.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.110
And the first one knocked at door at
this [INAUDIBLE] at the Bank of China.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.860
And then you starting to find that there
are opportunity to do financings for

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:41.720
private companies.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:46.950
And because of that, you're thinking
about whether you can be part of them.

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:52.220
At that time,
entrepreneurship was not a popular word.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.480
And people like myself say,
I don't have much opportunity to do that,

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.750
because you need a very
large amount of capital.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:06.980
I mean, the first batch of Chinese
private enterprises typically are in

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:12.696
the area like manufacturings,
transfer from the.

00:04:12.696 --> 00:04:18.350
The so-called county
enterprise before all real

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:23.630
estate developers who make
the first their goal.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.810
Then venture into other areas.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:29.314
But for myself who has not,

00:04:29.314 --> 00:04:34.870
do not have a lot of capital,
there's not much really I can do.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:36.910
Until Internet come to play.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:42.910
And I have the thank U.S.
for giving me the inspiration.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.440
I came to Silicon Valley very often,

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:51.460
because I have a lot of classmates who
are working in different tech firms here.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:59.460
&gt;From 1998 all the way to 2000, each
time when I came, I noticed big changes.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:03.440
Because many of those who work at Oracle,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.360
SAP, IBM,
before have switched to inside jobs.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:15.850
And when I'm walking through you know
all those on drive through 101, you see

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:21.530
the big billboard change from advertising
of big companies to Internet companies.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:25.730
So you see the Internet is really
changing how people live and

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:28.910
work here in U.S.
And naturally, you will think,

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:33.360
well if this going to be the case in US,
it should happen in China.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.950
So, I have to say the first batch
of internet entrepreneur in China,

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:41.210
whether it's Bioduel,
or Tenson, or C-trip,

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:45.490
will get inspired because these
successful models in the U.S., and

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:49.380
we know we can do similarly like how U.S.
counterparts.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.350
Basically not a lot of capital sell, but

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.890
you can use venture capital to
help you to grow your business.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:03.950
So my first venture as Sea Trip,
is exactly followed that route.

00:06:04.998 --> 00:06:09.470
It's been doin.g very good and
if experience could

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:15.130
change the chow industry so
much in U.S. where on in China.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:19.670
So that's how I started Sea Trip in 1999.

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:21.930
Frankly, it's a very difficult decision.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.110
If I'm a software engineer,

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:33.200
it's probably a natural extension
of my previous professions.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.470
I was a banker, right?

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:40.610
A banker provides advice to
the corporates in the merger and

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:43.440
acquisitions, debt financings, IPOs.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:50.800
And I changed my role completely
to something very, very new to me.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.180
And it was a very big venture for me.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:07:00.080
And the only thing I'm comfortable is
that, I know if I'm not successful.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.300
I can always go back,
because if Deutsche Bank does not hire me,

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.620
there's someone else going to hire me.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:09.360
Look back,
is actually not very good mentality.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.490
Because when you do
entrepreneurship you better be so

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:14.890
determined that you will
make it successful.

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:18.080
Not just to hoping that one
day you're going to go back.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.450
You should not leave yourself
any other possibilities.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.750
But, that's how [LAUGH] I started.

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:25.840
I do know I have possibilities.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.780
If I can be successful, and

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.950
I clear understand that there's
a good chance that I might not.

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:37.754
I can go back, and
maybe another bank going to hire me.

00:07:37.754 --> 00:07:43.530
But, when I really get involved
with It startup, you dont' have

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:49.190
time to think about anything, because you
want to survive and you want to do well.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:52.279
And everyday you're just so

00:07:52.279 --> 00:07:57.433
heavily engaged with
your surviving efforts.

00:07:57.433 --> 00:08:01.397
Indeed, I would say the first two years
you try to find your business model.

00:08:01.397 --> 00:08:03.355
You work day and night.

00:08:03.355 --> 00:08:08.270
You don't have time to even think
about where's my, as alternatives.

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:14.890
You don't, yeah, I actually,
just tell you guys a little story.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:18.500
When I resigned from Deutsch Bank,
my boss,

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.715
the head of Asia capital markets say,
Neil, you should stay four more months.

00:08:22.715 --> 00:08:24.830
I say why four more months?

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:30.510
Because euro is just
created around that time.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:33.990
And the euro was introduced
as a major currency.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.680
I was a fixed income banker, and

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:41.400
I had been covered minister of
finance of China for many years.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:48.230
And minister of finance had a plan
to go to euro, Europe too, actually.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.086
Our launch is first Europe bank.

00:08:50.086 --> 00:08:54.310
So my boss tell me, look, you haven't
covered the costs for so many years.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:58.360
It's your pride to actually
launch a Euro bank for

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:01.810
the Minister of Finance and
this is your country.

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:04.240
You would be so proud of doing that.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:05.299
Just wait for four months.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.020
And I check with my [INAUDIBLE] and
they say you can't wait for four months.

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.180
Entrepreneurship is about speed.

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:15.790
Another four months you
don't know what can happen.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:22.920
So I decide to quit in November, December
instead of wait until that spring in 2000.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:28.820
Interestingly, I never
requite that decision because

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:34.060
the Europe bond was not launch until yet
to solve [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:36.370
Europe will not doing well
as you guys all know, right?

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:39.070
And the Europe coming down and
the ministry finance, no,

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:40.490
no I don't want to do it.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:45.997
So they wait until 2002 or
2003 to actually launch the Euro bank.

00:09:45.997 --> 00:09:50.000
So I go back to tell my boss later become
an independent director of C-Trip.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.260
I say look, if I wait for four months, I'm
going to wait for another ten months, and

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:57.130
maybe two years and
there will never be a C-Trip.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:00.780
So if you like to do something,
if you really feel like there's

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:04.290
something calling you inside,
you better do it, right away.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:05.990
And that's about,
all about entrepreneurship.

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:11.130
&gt;&gt; So, you said that you could always go
back to banking if you weren't doing well.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:15.450
Well, you were doing very well at C-trip,
you started in 1999.

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:19.540
And in just four years,
before the Euro bank was launched,

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.470
you brought it public, listed on NASDAQ.

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:26.665
And whats amazing is it's not
even the only company you found

00:10:26.665 --> 00:10:28.730
a successful in that period.

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:33.530
In 2002 you found a Home Inns, now one
of the largest hotel chains in China.

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:37.450
We're in more than 300 cities,
close to 3,000 hotel chains.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:40.650
And also four years listed on NASDAQ,
how do you do it?

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:44.810
What do you think its contributed
to your success as entrepreneur?

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:49.200
&gt;&gt; I think it's very important that
once you get into the business

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:54.560
you are able to really
understand the sector very well.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.690
Instead of just looking
at your own business,

00:10:56.690 --> 00:11:00.410
and say, I'm doing my daily execution.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:05.160
So at a sea trip, it's really a very
eye opening opportunity for me, for

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.130
the whole hospitality industry.

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:07.780
You realize,

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:13.050
oh, the Chinese travel agencies are so
backwards in terms of their IT systems.

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.750
And there's so much middle man involved in

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:20.150
bringing a customer whether it's business
or leisure to the ultimate destinations.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:21.720
There's so much you can do.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:25.850
Obviously as a company
you can't just one issue.

00:11:25.850 --> 00:11:31.590
But you do get to know a lot
about the whole industry.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:38.880
One thing which we noticed, when I
walked in a sea trip is all upstream,

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:43.992
so called in the hotel industry itself
is again, very, very under developed.

00:11:43.992 --> 00:11:48.560
For example, there's many so

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:53.550
called business hotel or
economy hotel chains in the US,

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:59.150
but in China back in 2002 and
2003 there's almost none.

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:03.250
And it's actually shocking
to me that there's no

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:06.389
such hotel which catering
to a specific needs.

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:09.310
And when we launch [INAUDIBLE] initially,

00:12:09.310 --> 00:12:13.380
I was a little bit concerned
how big our company could be.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:18.650
Because each hotel is small,
100 room, 150 rooms,

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:23.540
and how big a company this could become,
or whether China's ready.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:29.070
But, again, I got inspiration from U.S.,
I remember I was in

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:36.020
Dallas visiting a few travel
service company because that was

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:41.160
the hub of many U.S.
travel service companies.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.930
And I arrive at the airport,

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:46.850
I rent a car, drive from the airport
all the way to downtown.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:50.450
It was like night time around 9 pm,
whatever.

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:58.340
And during that 20 to 30 minutes drive,
I observed there's about 15 different

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:03.620
names of budget hotels or
economy hotel chain.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:05.700
&gt;From Red Roof, all the way to Motel 6,

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.538
whatever, and the total number
of hotels I counted around 25.

00:13:09.538 --> 00:13:15.460
So, from Dallas,
from Suburban airport to the city, you

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:19.722
can have 25 hotels, so I say, well, market
size is not a problem for China now.

00:13:19.722 --> 00:13:21.460
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:22.610
&gt;&gt; That indeed,

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:27.580
I never imagine we can have
3,000 hotels In China.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:35.290
Of by all means, this all created by our
efforts and by many of our competitors,

00:13:35.290 --> 00:13:39.720
and the market is definitely there and
the demand is so big.

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.644
And we just need to create a good
product to cater to the users.

00:13:45.046 --> 00:13:48.790
&gt;&gt; To meet quite a few transitions
going from a PhD to a MBA and

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:51.210
then banking to entrepreneurship.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:56.540
In 2005, you make yet
another big decision going from

00:13:56.540 --> 00:14:01.020
being an entrepreneur to becoming
the entrepreneur behind entrepreneurs.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:05.470
And what's really interesting is you
could have chosen to start your own fun,

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:08.380
which I'm sure you have
plenty of resources to do.

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:12.200
But instead, you decided to be
a franchise with Sequioa Capital

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.720
was really well established
American brand.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:18.560
What was some of this considerations
behind bringing this global

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:20.120
brand to China?

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:27.090
&gt;&gt; Well, I think, first of all,
if you look at my career, from PhD proven.

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:31.240
I never got a PhD to MBAs, or not MBAs,

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:36.170
MPPMs and to banking etc,, I don't
think we had many choices back then.

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:42.240
And this is something which you guys
compared with us, you guys are so lucky.

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:48.287
I'm talk about especially those
public students from China ,because

00:14:48.287 --> 00:14:53.790
when I arrive in New York, I don't think I
can get into business school right away.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:57.730
And again, of the business school,
you don't have many choices either.

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:03.250
And oftentimes, you never go into
internship or prior working experience,

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:05.838
so can tell you what's your best fit for
you.

00:15:05.838 --> 00:15:10.810
But what actually drives me to make

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:15.080
those choices is that you
see the great opportunity.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.210
And also, you go with the whole track.

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:23.348
In the last 20, 30 the big trend
is that you think that China

00:15:23.348 --> 00:15:30.520
is going to become a global economy,
a powerhouse.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:37.110
And if you believe in that and
in a poker term you call it,

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:39.930
if you are owing China you
are largely doing well.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:45.370
And I guess that's probably going to be
the case for the next ten or 20 years.

00:15:45.370 --> 00:15:50.368
If you, so called, can do or
if you belong in China, or

00:15:50.368 --> 00:15:55.601
even more so, all-in China,
you are likely to do well.

00:15:55.601 --> 00:15:59.037
And coming back to doing venture capital,

00:15:59.037 --> 00:16:04.001
I always think that probably
the best before me after spending

00:16:04.001 --> 00:16:08.309
seven years at the bank,
and then another six years.

00:16:08.309 --> 00:16:12.858
As an entrepreneur is getting to
investment field because you can

00:16:12.858 --> 00:16:18.620
leverage your experience both as
a financier as well as an entrepreneur.

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:22.661
So that's something which I
think public fit me very well.

00:16:22.661 --> 00:16:27.357
Clearly I never planned that I never
get in the bank and say one day I do

00:16:27.357 --> 00:16:32.685
entrepreneurship and then I can best
fit myself as a venture capitalist.

00:16:32.685 --> 00:16:33.915
I never do that.

00:16:33.915 --> 00:16:39.495
Say I'm on a sea trip I only want to
survive and make sea trip a great company.

00:16:39.495 --> 00:16:43.587
Never think that okay,
this [INAUDIBLE] can position

00:16:43.587 --> 00:16:48.331
myself be a good venture
capitalist sometime down the road.

00:16:48.331 --> 00:16:52.350
Now, I can do for myself.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:56.212
Indeed, I make some personal
investment while at sea trip and

00:16:56.212 --> 00:17:00.260
some of those are very successful
like folks media, E-House.

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:05.320
But I was thinking how I can so
the best leverage,

00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:09.400
in my experience, at the same time
can really make an investment and

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:13.302
have a big impact on the whole

00:17:13.302 --> 00:17:18.336
so-called new economy.

00:17:18.336 --> 00:17:25.330
You can set up, for myself but,
I think it's can be grow,

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:31.040
public not as fast compared with
setting up something with Sequoia.

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:34.880
And also, I feel like I really
want to learn from what

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:39.920
a firm like Sequoia has been
accumulating in the last 40 years.

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:47.860
In Silicon Valley, there's a many firms
has been active in the investment space,

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:51.570
but Sequoia has been,
obviously, the longest, and

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:56.990
still probably the best or
I will say at least one of the best.

00:17:56.990 --> 00:18:02.100
So, I was very intrigued how they can do
that, one generation after the other.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:05.400
So, three,

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:09.166
to raise money to make investment is
probably not the most difficult thing.

00:18:09.166 --> 00:18:14.440
But, so China seems how to
make a sustainable business?

00:18:14.440 --> 00:18:19.310
To me, I think Sequoia,
China is just like another venture.

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:23.430
If you want to make one or
two good investments, that's an easy job.

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:24.984
But how you can build a team?

00:18:24.984 --> 00:18:26.913
And how to make a sensible business?

00:18:26.913 --> 00:18:30.990
Just like what i did at sea trip I think
that's a very very different thing.

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:36.490
And today look back, after ten years,
I'm very happy the choice i made.

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:41.090
Because on our ten year anniversary,

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:45.720
we have allowed this entrepreneur,
which becomes Sequoia portfolio of CEOs.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:51.890
But more importantly, from ten years ago,
a three person, four person team,

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:57.250
today we have 12 partners, and
we have cover some of the most important

00:18:57.250 --> 00:19:02.030
set us in China and become investors
in many of the leading companies.

00:19:02.030 --> 00:19:05.850
And that's something which is probably
difficult to achieve just with one man's

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:07.468
efforts or with friends.

00:19:09.174 --> 00:19:12.413
&gt;&gt; So on that topic,
Sequoia capital in China, for

00:19:12.413 --> 00:19:16.540
the last ten years,
has a phenomenal track record.

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:21.054
It pretty much put money in all of
China's biggest unicorns, Alibaba,

00:19:21.054 --> 00:19:25.480
JD.com, Vipshop,
people call you Trainus Midas.

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:27.710
You turn stone to gold.

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:32.100
What do you think makes you so successful
as an investor, especially in China?

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:34.210
What's your secret sauce?

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:40.090
&gt;&gt; Well I think this venture
capitol investment is,

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:43.090
as a professional always has a regret.

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:46.360
Clearly you have lot of very
successful companies but

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:50.940
obviously we also have a lot of regret and
frankly mistakes.

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:55.490
And the good thing is you
learn from your mistakes and

00:19:55.490 --> 00:19:57.890
then you try to improve every year.

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:03.124
The other thing very important is
when you look at the Sequoia US,

00:20:03.124 --> 00:20:05.419
they have 48 high street.

00:20:05.419 --> 00:20:07.540
They have long list of success.

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:12.451
Oracle, Cisco, Apple and

00:20:12.451 --> 00:20:17.152
then Google, et cetera.

00:20:17.152 --> 00:20:24.280
And one of reason behind that is they
able to capture all the major trend.

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:28.189
And they're able to say no to some trend

00:20:28.189 --> 00:20:32.690
which turned out to be
just pure illusions and

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:37.783
that I think is probably
the most important factors

00:20:37.783 --> 00:20:42.061
behind the success of firms like Sequoia.

00:20:42.061 --> 00:20:46.899
And see, for us in China,
if there's any kind of

00:20:46.899 --> 00:20:52.209
so-called success for us,
then that's, I think,

00:20:52.209 --> 00:20:57.170
we have been really taking
the major trend right.

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:05.000
I remember back seven years ago, we have
decided that we should spend enough time,

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:10.150
and put enough investment
into e-commerce area.

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:14.310
We do have a view that e-commerce will
become very, very important in China.

00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:18.380
Probably even more important,

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:23.020
compared with retail space in the US.

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:27.370
So we amassed probably around
15 e-commerce company in China.

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:31.880
Some of those are not successful,
but we did capture the top

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:36.640
five to six very successful
e-commerce in China.

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:38.390
Same for O to O.

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:43.410
Offline to online is not a big space
in the US, but it was a big space,

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:46.260
and continued to be a very
important space for China.

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:53.490
We think this is a very important area for
us.

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:57.080
And we did a lot of research back four or
five years ago.

00:21:57.080 --> 00:22:00.390
We invest probably around
again 12 to 15 company.

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:04.210
And we happened to capture
the very top four companies,

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:08.010
from Mai Twan to Jan Ping
to Otama to Da Ta.

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:13.530
And that's the way I think probably
good firms should make investments.

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:17.340
You actually have a view on the market,
and

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:20.590
hopefully a little bit earlier
than most other people.

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:25.000
And you put big emphasize on the sectors,
and

00:22:25.000 --> 00:22:29.880
making multiple investments
in that sectors.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:31.938
And this is same for US.

00:22:31.938 --> 00:22:36.080
If you look at Sequoia,
back in 40 years ago,

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:41.010
they pay big attention
to summer conductors.

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:45.600
And then summer conductor
leads to personal computer.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:49.679
And they again, invest multiple
company in personal computer.

00:22:49.679 --> 00:22:52.082
And then to software companies.

00:22:52.082 --> 00:22:55.671
And then,
where internet comes into the major scene,

00:22:55.671 --> 00:22:59.460
they invest multiple internet companies.

00:22:59.460 --> 00:23:02.220
And then going to mobile internet.

00:23:02.220 --> 00:23:06.436
That is the way I think a good
investor should follow.

00:23:06.436 --> 00:23:10.090
The I'm about it.

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:16.660
There always something which you will
missed, because of judgement on person,

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:20.670
or judgement on particular business model,
but that's okay.

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:22.830
You always will make mistake, but

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:27.560
as long as you capture all the major
trends, you'll be doing very, very fine.

00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:32.260
And sometimes you are including
to say no to some sectors.

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:36.794
I remember seven,
eight years ago is a buzz

00:23:36.794 --> 00:23:41.646
word both here in the US as
well as in China and luckily,

00:23:41.646 --> 00:23:48.310
our US partners as well as our China
partners decided this is not a big area.

00:23:48.310 --> 00:23:52.912
Fundamentally it's not easy to create
large market caps by investing

00:23:52.912 --> 00:23:57.190
venture capital or
even private equities into the sectors.

00:23:57.190 --> 00:24:00.513
Indeed, after ten years
anyone can remember

00:24:00.513 --> 00:24:05.415
like what are the top multi-billion
dollar company of the space?

00:24:05.415 --> 00:24:06.494
Nothing, all right.

00:24:06.494 --> 00:24:11.572
And avoid those sort of pitfalls is
again very, very important, because.

00:24:11.572 --> 00:24:18.070
There are certain firms spend one third
of their time and money into the space.

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:20.430
And they end up finding that
there is not much there.

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:28.800
I think it's probably
the most important efforts.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:33.580
And, you know, I'm and judgemental costs.

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:38.680
Coming down to companies,
you always will make mistakes.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:46.360
I remember, when we look at
the video space back nine years ago.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:48.390
We liked the space.

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:51.250
And we actually put a lot of effort on it.

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:56.850
And U.S. so far has invested in YouTube,
very successful.

00:24:56.850 --> 00:25:04.120
We picked the number three player in the
space, and it's just a small game for us.

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:09.995
And these things will happen all the time,
because you may not be,

00:25:09.995 --> 00:25:15.120
do all right, for
judging a particular entrepreneur.

00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:16.600
In that particular case,

00:25:16.600 --> 00:25:20.120
the companies found that it was
not very good at marketing, and

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:25.040
happen to be that marketing is the key
efforts for a video sharing companies.

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:30.560
And sometimes you get lucky,
like in our case VIP shop, or JD, or

00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:34.630
you know recent case like a made.

00:25:34.630 --> 00:25:38.780
But the big trend is a lot more
important to calling right

00:25:38.780 --> 00:25:39.860
on one particular company.

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:43.710
&gt;&gt; So what are doing on some of
these major trends, going forward?

00:25:43.710 --> 00:25:45.310
What excites you about China today?

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:53.090
&gt;&gt; I think every couple of year there
always some technology breakthroughs.

00:25:53.090 --> 00:25:59.260
And the last five years in the U.S., the
big scene is big data star enterprises.

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:01.930
And is not a big scene in China as yet.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.580
But I think it's becoming
increasing important.

00:26:04.580 --> 00:26:09.630
In fact, I was think the returnees

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:12.340
from this part of world is
very free from Silicon Valley.

00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:15.820
Might have a better role to play in China.

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:19.210
If you talk about big data and
software enterprises.

00:26:19.210 --> 00:26:24.190
China, in this time, is like U.S.,
five, six years ago.

00:26:24.190 --> 00:26:29.080
Where you come down to enterprises
softwares, sans enterprises and big datas.

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:35.263
So I think this is actually a great
opportunity and just like 1999,

00:26:35.263 --> 00:26:41.000
there's many big internet companies U.S.
being founded.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:44.530
China, there's very few areas
that have been covered, and

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:47.050
this is very much comparable.

00:26:47.050 --> 00:26:49.800
And when you come down to, for

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:56.270
the attorneys to go back
to a founding company.

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:58.330
I think this is actually a very good area.

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:03.010
Because if you talk about consumer
Internet, you may not be able to

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:07.120
compete successfully against so
called the local guy.

00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:13.940
Because in the consumer Internet space,
the culture factor is so heavy.

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:16.660
You have to understand
your user very well.

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:20.570
If you work in the U.S.
for too long you may not do that.

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:25.850
If you look at it like Charleston,
and you feel like Robin Lee.

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:27.690
When they go back to China,

00:27:27.690 --> 00:27:31.770
they have a very long working
experience here in the U.S..

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:34.965
They're not like an early employer or

00:27:34.965 --> 00:27:38.940
an early employer of Google to go
back to funding Chinese companies.

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:44.260
But in the enterprise area, the technology
component is more important.

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:47.658
So people work here for
many years in that space,

00:27:47.658 --> 00:27:53.040
could actually play a very important role,
in shaping up the space in China.

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:56.230
So I think this is definitely
a very important area.

00:27:56.230 --> 00:28:01.827
The other important area is so
called robotics or

00:28:01.827 --> 00:28:06.470
artificial areas, again In that area,

00:28:06.470 --> 00:28:12.780
there are many U.S.
company, ahead of on China.

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:19.520
But China started to see some local
born companies which had developed.

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:22.260
Developing it's own technology,
company like DGI.

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:22.930
Right?
You know,

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:25.960
definitely the number one
player in the drone space.

00:28:25.960 --> 00:28:30.460
And so I think we'll probably
see some interesting dynamics,

00:28:30.460 --> 00:28:32.950
even the Chinese company will

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:40.180
doing better than some of the U.S.
company addressing those major trends.

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:43.860
But you know just like
what we have seen before.

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:46.640
Every couple of years there's
new technology break through and

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:49.930
bringing new commercial opportunity.

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:54.946
But relatively speaking I
think China's opportunity

00:28:54.946 --> 00:28:58.768
may be bigger when compared with U.S..

00:28:58.768 --> 00:29:03.962
Because the so-called,
the efficiency in the tradition

00:29:03.962 --> 00:29:08.626
industry is a world behind,
compared with U.S..

00:29:08.626 --> 00:29:15.670
That's why e-commerce could do so
well in China compared with the US.

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:17.274
And same for.

00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:22.580
So, while we're on the topic of
American companies in China.

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:24.050
China's a huge market.

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:24.640
Growing.

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:27.220
Second largest economy in the world.

00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:30.630
And Sakuya China has actually also
been working with a lot of these

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.210
American companies.

00:29:32.210 --> 00:29:36.410
You've investing in LinkedIn,
you're a strategic partner with Airbnb.

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:39.790
So for you, when did you have some
of these biggest opportunities,

00:29:39.790 --> 00:29:43.650
especially challenges to American
companies entering China?

00:29:43.650 --> 00:29:46.580
And what Sakuya's role in
helping navigate this?

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:52.090
&gt;&gt; If you look at global companies,
especially U.S. companies coming to China.

00:29:52.090 --> 00:29:54.279
I'd say in the first 30 years,

00:29:54.279 --> 00:29:58.420
40 years, when China opened door
&gt;&gt; Many U.S.

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:00.480
company has been very successful.

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:03.380
Because the technology is so advanced,

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:06.450
that when you're trying,
there's no competitions.

00:30:06.450 --> 00:30:09.020
Like when GM come to China,
people love it.

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:14.285
Even though GM might not be the best
automobile company in the world.

00:30:14.285 --> 00:30:14.805
Right?

00:30:14.805 --> 00:30:17.300
And a lot of joint ventures get setup.

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:19.430
So, at that time, you know,

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:25.188
the key word in a joint venture is
use technology to gain market share.

00:30:25.188 --> 00:30:26.187
Right.

00:30:26.187 --> 00:30:30.690
[FOREIGN]
So whether it's European players or

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:36.210
American player, often times set
up their own subsidiaries or

00:30:36.210 --> 00:30:38.100
set up a joint venture in China.

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:41.130
And they could do very well
against the competition.

00:30:43.070 --> 00:30:48.600
Either against the private company,
or against the state enterprises.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:53.450
But since has been really changed,
many sectors today,

00:30:53.450 --> 00:30:58.070
the private companies in China and still
enterprise in China are very competitive.

00:30:58.070 --> 00:31:01.610
They have learned,
they are developing their own ideas.

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:05.040
And they can really compete very
effectively with you, right?

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:08.710
When PNG goes to China,
when GM goes to China back 30 years ago.

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:10.240
They dominate certain space.

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:15.520
Today, they have to compete
fiercely with many local players.

00:31:15.520 --> 00:31:21.250
So one of the ideas here is that,
if a U.S. company comes to China.

00:31:21.250 --> 00:31:27.200
Just copy what they have in done in U.S.,
it may not be that effect to anymore.

00:31:27.200 --> 00:31:33.376
So you have to have to change your model,
your product to cater to China's market.

00:31:33.376 --> 00:31:37.665
Very unique business involvement.

00:31:37.665 --> 00:31:39.310
Especially in the internet space.

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:45.280
Because internet, for internet, China and
U.S. start around the same time.

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:51.663
For some business model,
China even advance compared with U.S.

00:31:51.663 --> 00:31:54.270
for some example today,

00:31:54.270 --> 00:31:59.560
clearly much better than some of those
social network companies in China.

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:05.593
You can't say learn from Facebook or
copycat Facebook.

00:32:05.593 --> 00:32:09.476
In fact, if you look at Facebook and
you know, many others,

00:32:09.476 --> 00:32:13.066
some of those features are run
from Chinese companies.

00:32:13.066 --> 00:32:16.155
So if a internet company in U.S.
want to go to China,

00:32:16.155 --> 00:32:20.720
they have to think about whether
they have a edge there or not.

00:32:20.720 --> 00:32:25.780
Secondly, they going to have to face
the competition from local companies,

00:32:25.780 --> 00:32:28.790
many driven by very,
very strong entrepreneurs.

00:32:28.790 --> 00:32:30.040
So how are you going to be successful?

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:38.090
One thing which we suggest, is to having
a very unique share holding structures.

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:44.013
You should introduce someone as
entrepreneur, as in your home country.

00:32:44.013 --> 00:32:48.566
Someone not listen to the head office, but

00:32:48.566 --> 00:32:52.488
someone able to based his product and

00:32:52.488 --> 00:32:58.447
service around Chinese
unique business involvement.

00:32:58.447 --> 00:33:02.040
And leverage the edge from head office.

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:05.530
That's how we did it on
the LinkedIn ventures and

00:33:05.530 --> 00:33:09.570
that's sort of why I'll try very hard for
NBNB.

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:13.010
The product of LinkedIn and
NBMB are wonderful,

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:16.500
there's no question about it, but
there are some local competitors.

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:18.970
Their CEOs and
their farmers are very strong.

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:24.490
And you need to have a structure
where the guy who run

00:33:24.490 --> 00:33:29.570
link in China has a direct
interest in running that business.

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:34.030
So, the management team need
to be compensated properly.

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:39.500
And not just compensated at a head office,
and I think that's probably the best

00:33:39.500 --> 00:33:44.390
way for a U.S. company to compete,
and do well in China.

00:33:44.390 --> 00:33:47.290
In fact, if a Chinese company
come to Silicon Valley,

00:33:47.290 --> 00:33:49.120
they should do the same thing, right?

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:52.820
If they send someone from head office and

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:57.880
try to compete with local company here
in US, I don't think they have a chance.

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:02.520
But they have to find the best
talent in Silicon Valley and

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:06.300
give them enough economic interest,
let them run the show.

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:09.443
And then leverage the back end from China,
and

00:34:09.443 --> 00:34:12.920
then you might have a chance
of competing locally.

00:34:12.920 --> 00:34:16.360
It is the model moving
forward I propose for

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:19.680
both US company as well for
Chinese companies.

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:22.170
So that's how we actually
being LinkedIn to China and

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:24.390
that's how we bring NBNB China.

00:34:24.390 --> 00:34:29.760
And I think this is probably a model
that we'll see if this fits for

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:31.830
many other US companies.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:37.142
Even though, they have already very,
very successful here on US.

00:34:37.142 --> 00:34:41.893
And as the business partner
what do we bring to a table is

00:34:41.893 --> 00:34:45.660
that we helping to get the best team.

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:49.220
And also to put all
resources behind it and

00:34:49.220 --> 00:34:52.450
to make sure that you have the right
partners for your efforts.

00:34:52.450 --> 00:34:57.560
And actually it's very, it's not easy
to find such so called local CEOs.

00:34:57.560 --> 00:35:00.490
Because he need to be very entrepreneur,
as I mentioned.

00:35:00.490 --> 00:35:06.619
At the same time, he have to understand
how to working with a large organization.

00:35:06.619 --> 00:35:09.916
Because you do want to
leverage your head office.

00:35:09.916 --> 00:35:14.175
Some of the existing
technology infrastructures.

00:35:14.175 --> 00:35:19.320
And someone who knows how to make
sure the respective department

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:23.805
within your head office can
work with you very closely.

00:35:23.805 --> 00:35:29.740
So it's difficult to
find this type of talent.

00:35:29.740 --> 00:35:32.241
It's a tough job.

00:35:32.241 --> 00:35:33.585
And we have been trying very hard.

00:35:33.585 --> 00:35:37.750
And so far, we have been quite
successful with Lincoln and

00:35:37.750 --> 00:35:42.220
hopefully we can extend
it to other US companies.

00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:45.180
&gt;&gt; Definitely want to
stay in airbnbs in China.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:47.840
So now two questions from the audience.

00:35:47.840 --> 00:35:50.760
We have Maria and Neve on either side.

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:53.970
Please raise your hand high if you
are interested in asking a question so

00:35:53.970 --> 00:35:54.660
they can spot you.

00:35:54.660 --> 00:35:59.800
You can also tweet to our #stanfordgss
if you have a question and

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:01.900
I think we'll start with
a question from Twitter.

00:36:01.900 --> 00:36:05.420
&gt;&gt; Right, so thank you both for
such an inspirational discussion.

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:08.250
Now the first question from Twitter.

00:36:08.250 --> 00:36:11.210
Many of us here aspire
to be entrepreneurs and

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:14.560
see China as a great market opportunity,
but

00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:17.790
what are some of the unique challenges
that entrepreneurs face in China?

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:21.190
And how do you recommend we overcome them?

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:27.900
&gt;&gt; I think one of the most frequently
encountered challenges is competition.

00:36:29.000 --> 00:36:30.060
It's actually interesting.

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:34.180
I'm always very puzzled and
I still do not have the perfect answer.

00:36:34.180 --> 00:36:36.751
Here in the US, in Silicon Valley,
there's one business model.

00:36:36.751 --> 00:36:43.683
Typically, no one either care to copy,
or no really need to copy.

00:36:43.683 --> 00:36:48.276
You know you have Uber,
you have airbnb you

00:36:48.276 --> 00:36:54.130
have LinkedIn, there's very few copycats.

00:36:54.130 --> 00:36:55.770
In China,

00:36:55.770 --> 00:36:59.850
as long as your business is doing well
you will definitely have copycats.

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:03.570
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's a matter of how to compete more

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:04.831
successfully.

00:37:04.831 --> 00:37:08.013
And that actually is putting pressure on

00:37:08.013 --> 00:37:11.670
the leaders to innovate all the time,
right.

00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:13.507
I remember when I was running C Trip.

00:37:13.507 --> 00:37:18.072
One of the, my routine job is
come to Monday meeting and

00:37:18.072 --> 00:37:23.840
discuss how our competitor was doing,
especially Eelung, right?

00:37:23.840 --> 00:37:29.620
So one of the Monday discussion
items is spending 30 minutes,

00:37:29.620 --> 00:37:33.560
and discuss what Eelung have
launched in the last one week.

00:37:33.560 --> 00:37:40.370
And indeed, I have to say many of those
are copied, what set you've been doing.

00:37:40.370 --> 00:37:43.140
Some are very detailed, like marketing,

00:37:43.140 --> 00:37:48.170
plans, like sending carts at the airport
when we do that they get back.

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:50.130
So, okay to do right,

00:37:50.130 --> 00:37:53.739
you just have to innovate all the time
to make sure you stay ahead of the game.

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:58.110
And it's a rarity, and
people have to face.

00:37:58.110 --> 00:38:01.920
Many business models you can not IP it,

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:07.100
and you just have to raise more money.

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:10.140
You just have to innovate all the time.

00:38:10.140 --> 00:38:14.390
And you just have to differentiate
yourself well enough and

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:18.870
that differentiation sometimes
coming down through very details.

00:38:18.870 --> 00:38:22.240
When we look at Citribus and eLong,

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:26.440
at that time we are around
two times bigger than eLong.

00:38:26.440 --> 00:38:31.100
And one would differentiate we
have is through call center.

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:32.409
We provided better services.

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:35.660
That's actually become a big edge.

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:39.800
And if you come down to
merchant relationship,

00:38:39.800 --> 00:38:44.415
when we establish certain relationship
with a hotel group, they follow.

00:38:44.415 --> 00:38:47.805
That relationship,

00:38:47.805 --> 00:38:52.440
it's difficult to exclusive deals.

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:55.740
But only thing which has been
done well in differential sale

00:38:55.740 --> 00:38:58.100
is a better customer services.

00:38:58.100 --> 00:39:02.579
And so you can call that small details but

00:39:02.579 --> 00:39:07.200
that small detail bring very big change.

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:11.122
Bring very big gap of four
Citrum against eLong.

00:39:11.122 --> 00:39:16.440
And so for China, if you want to get into
those competitive space, you have to.

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:18.880
Be prepared and to innovate all the times.

00:39:20.470 --> 00:39:26.430
And the other thing that I want to
probably point out is that it's

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:29.290
difficult to retain
talents in Silicon Valley,

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:32.540
because people are going
to strike out on their own.

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:35.420
It's even more difficult to do so
in China.

00:39:35.420 --> 00:39:40.810
If you're looking at a turn over rate
at a major Internet companies in China,

00:39:40.810 --> 00:39:44.950
it's actually higher,
a lot higher compare with US companies.

00:39:44.950 --> 00:39:47.060
Many company feel like you know.

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:52.066
Sorry, many senior executive feel like,
you know to do startup today.

00:39:52.066 --> 00:39:54.122
You know, the environment's so good.

00:39:54.122 --> 00:40:01.160
There's so many people, so
much money, a chase after them.

00:40:01.160 --> 00:40:04.138
And they,
especially in the mobile Internet space,

00:40:04.138 --> 00:40:07.461
the entry barrier to start up
something is very, is lower.

00:40:07.461 --> 00:40:13.053
So, we do see people willing to consider,
do something on his own.

00:40:13.053 --> 00:40:15.309
Instead of staying with a large company.

00:40:15.309 --> 00:40:19.110
And so I think that's a challenge,
that how to retain people.

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:25.870
And obviously compensation is one way,
ESOP, etc.

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:30.510
But also to build a very strong culture to
make sure that your top people could stay.

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:37.588
&gt;&gt; Thank you for sharing with us.
I'm Talbe, MBA 1.

00:40:37.588 --> 00:40:42.073
So I'm really curious to see how you can
evaluate a company with a trend that is

00:40:42.073 --> 00:40:43.252
kind of uncertain.

00:40:43.252 --> 00:40:45.875
So earlier you mentioned that
big data is one of the trends.

00:40:45.875 --> 00:40:48.338
And within big data there's going to
be a lot of different sectors.

00:40:48.338 --> 00:40:50.587
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Medicine, education, you name it.

00:40:50.587 --> 00:40:55.352
Can you maybe walk through us,
share some insights on how you

00:40:55.352 --> 00:41:00.399
would actually have quantifiable
metrics within those big data

00:41:00.399 --> 00:41:06.040
sectors to determine which one
would be potentially successful?

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:06.962
&gt;&gt; I think there's a few common,

00:41:06.962 --> 00:41:13.080
sort of check in point we will look at
when we're looking at any start-up.

00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:15.880
This is the same, whether it's
customer internet or with a price.

00:41:15.880 --> 00:41:19.580
One is obviously,
the background of the team,

00:41:19.580 --> 00:41:23.880
whether it actually fits for
that type of entrepreneurship.

00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:26.660
For example, if it's e-commerce,

00:41:26.660 --> 00:41:31.680
I will be very eager to know whether
he has a consumer retail experience.

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:34.220
Whether he had supply chain experience.

00:41:34.220 --> 00:41:39.260
But for such enterprises, I will look
to see if he has the relevant software

00:41:39.260 --> 00:41:44.870
experience or
enterprise experience before.

00:41:44.870 --> 00:41:46.909
Secondly, I will also look at market size.

00:41:47.990 --> 00:41:53.161
That's actually very very important,
if you look at e-commerce area,

00:41:53.161 --> 00:41:58.780
the reason why JD become one of
the top internet companies in China,

00:41:58.780 --> 00:42:03.170
because they address the most
important area at the very beginning.

00:42:03.170 --> 00:42:09.751
The three C's, the consumer,
the [INAUDIBLE] electronics.

00:42:09.751 --> 00:42:12.134
If you start from something else.

00:42:12.134 --> 00:42:14.510
For example you found
some books they want.

00:42:14.510 --> 00:42:17.180
That you may not get as a kid as you grow.

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:22.390
So the total [INAUDIBLE]
market always something

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:25.680
very important especially in
the first one to two years.

00:42:25.680 --> 00:42:30.340
You can always expand from your core
areas after you've become successful.

00:42:31.500 --> 00:42:37.370
So in the southern parts of the area,
you have to start from some verticals.

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:40.660
And the question is,
whether that vertical is big enough for

00:42:40.660 --> 00:42:43.630
you to become a very substantial
leader and you can expand from that.

00:42:44.790 --> 00:42:49.406
Beyond these two point,
the other very important I think

00:42:49.406 --> 00:42:53.082
area to look after is
whether the time is right.

00:42:53.082 --> 00:43:01.228
Because enterprises demand
in China is clearly there.

00:43:01.228 --> 00:43:06.040
But people have priorities for
particular corporates, they look at it.

00:43:06.040 --> 00:43:10.850
How much money I will spend
on any different software?

00:43:10.850 --> 00:43:14.440
And what are the key sort of
area I want put money this year

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:16.840
against a year later or two year later?

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:21.450
So for any type of software
you should look at those

00:43:21.450 --> 00:43:26.326
area which corporate are having
the most pain in increasing efficiency.

00:43:26.326 --> 00:43:30.420
We look at securities, we look at storage,

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:35.000
we look at performance areas,
we look at HRIS.

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:38.100
Some of those areas in which clearly

00:43:38.100 --> 00:43:42.320
with dividing labor costs you
have to address in those issues.

00:43:42.320 --> 00:43:47.655
And I think that's probably an important
point for venture capitalists,

00:43:47.655 --> 00:43:52.835
as well as for entrepreneur who wants
to start a business in that sector.

00:43:52.835 --> 00:43:56.298
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Mr for your speeches,
it's very inspiring.

00:43:56.298 --> 00:43:58.570
So my question is about people.

00:43:58.570 --> 00:44:03.350
When you were about to start
how did you find your partners?

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:07.585
Because without good partners is
impossible for startups is successful.

00:44:07.585 --> 00:44:12.254
And the same as here, like, now if we
want to build up a startup in china,

00:44:12.254 --> 00:44:16.975
what's the most effective way
to find the perfect partners?

00:44:16.975 --> 00:44:18.085
Thank you.

00:44:18.085 --> 00:44:20.645
&gt;&gt; It's difficult to find
perfect partners, but

00:44:20.645 --> 00:44:23.745
as long as you find good partners,
that's okay.

00:44:23.745 --> 00:44:29.260
When I look at many startups,
when they walked into our conference

00:44:29.260 --> 00:44:33.889
room to present, to be honest,
most of the team at the so

00:44:33.889 --> 00:44:39.502
called anxious stage or serious stage
to not have a complete team but

00:44:39.502 --> 00:44:45.335
you need to have some co-team member and
addressing the major needs.

00:44:45.335 --> 00:44:49.517
In base we have myself and James and

00:44:49.517 --> 00:44:53.594
Chichi frankly is a little crowded team.

00:44:53.594 --> 00:44:57.504
We have guy already have a lot of
experience in different business

00:44:57.504 --> 00:45:02.090
segment but even with that three
people we just have to find Famine.

00:45:02.090 --> 00:45:06.140
Well who has worked at the travel
industry for many years.

00:45:06.140 --> 00:45:11.170
Because we do realize online
travel is about internet,

00:45:11.170 --> 00:45:13.130
but at the same time it's about travel.

00:45:13.130 --> 00:45:16.330
So we want someone who understand
the travel industry a lot.

00:45:16.330 --> 00:45:19.045
About how to build
a relationship with hotels?

00:45:19.045 --> 00:45:24.550
How to position ourselves against all
the other traditional travel agencies?

00:45:24.550 --> 00:45:27.890
So these are very interesting
questions that you have to address and

00:45:27.890 --> 00:45:33.170
you find someone who can complement your
skill set and to addressing those issues.

00:45:33.170 --> 00:45:38.660
And if you're going to have a so-called
core team at first, not a complete team,

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:43.914
then try to find what are the most
critical component of your starter

00:45:43.914 --> 00:45:49.810
like e-commerce then you have to better
have someone who understand supply chain.

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:54.630
If you do not have someone experienced
in that segment you don't even

00:45:55.760 --> 00:45:58.350
have the business taking off but

00:45:58.350 --> 00:46:03.160
maybe someone who do a better job
doing online marketing come second.

00:46:03.160 --> 00:46:07.320
It's still very important, but
it's not the most critical at the day one.

00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:10.380
So I think you just have to go

00:46:10.380 --> 00:46:15.040
along with your business ramp-up
to find that team gradually.

00:46:15.040 --> 00:46:17.650
Day one to find a complete
team is always challenge.

00:46:19.420 --> 00:46:21.591
&gt;&gt; I think we have time for
one last question from the audience.

00:46:21.591 --> 00:46:23.337
&gt;&gt; Hi Neil, thanks for your time.

00:46:23.337 --> 00:46:26.001
My question is which is a bigger bubble?

00:46:26.001 --> 00:46:27.541
China or Silicon Valley?

00:46:27.541 --> 00:46:28.765
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I want to be more,

00:46:28.765 --> 00:46:30.930
I actually want to be more specific.

00:46:30.930 --> 00:46:32.340
So here's a though experiment.

00:46:32.340 --> 00:46:37.780
If Uber raises another round right now,
and if the latest

00:46:37.780 --> 00:46:41.600
round investors are guaranteed a three
times return, would you be an investor?

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:42.820
Thanks.

00:46:42.820 --> 00:46:46.820
&gt;&gt; I don't want to comment
specifically on the Uber case, but

00:46:46.820 --> 00:46:52.080
I don't see a so-called big bubble there,
because at

00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:57.360
the end of the day you ask yourself,
are those startups really creating values?

00:46:57.360 --> 00:46:59.770
If they do create values, and

00:46:59.770 --> 00:47:05.280
most of the startup companies are creating
values, I'm not particularly worried.

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:08.670
Valuation, there's always ups and downs.

00:47:08.670 --> 00:47:12.310
If you look at a popular market,
there's always ups and downs anyway.

00:47:12.310 --> 00:47:15.610
And so I would not particularly
worry about valuation itself.

00:47:15.610 --> 00:47:19.110
Because the market itself will adjust.

00:47:19.110 --> 00:47:20.160
The venture capitalist,

00:47:20.160 --> 00:47:25.350
in general, I'm not stupid enough
to pay a premium consistently.

00:47:25.350 --> 00:47:30.100
They might pay premium for a short period
of time, but they will adjust themselves.

00:47:30.100 --> 00:47:33.680
And when you look at the so-called bubble,

00:47:35.710 --> 00:47:39.821
most of the companies
are just creating a lot of

00:47:39.821 --> 00:47:46.240
so-called users without providing value
to users that's something which I worry.

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:48.584
But if you look at UBER and ABNB, right?

00:47:48.584 --> 00:47:53.355
I mean clearly these are two
of the startup companies

00:47:53.355 --> 00:47:56.292
which become very successful.

00:47:56.292 --> 00:47:57.952
They do create a lot of values, right?

00:47:57.952 --> 00:48:03.175
I mean UBER clearly has
provide the most efficiency,

00:48:03.175 --> 00:48:07.819
the most efficient
transportation systems for

00:48:07.819 --> 00:48:14.214
all the people around the world and
ABNB has clearly new demands.

00:48:14.214 --> 00:48:18.768
And something which the hotel industry
has never even thought about.

00:48:18.768 --> 00:48:26.340
So I think these are either creating new
demand or increase efficiency big time.

00:48:26.340 --> 00:48:30.360
Ultimately, the value will be
created by those companies.

00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:34.080
I'm not particularly worried that if
there is any so called in a bubble

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:38.750
because the company my ahead of
itself a little bit for variation.

00:48:38.750 --> 00:48:43.160
If you look at China back in 1999,

00:48:43.160 --> 00:48:47.894
where there's a bunch of
companies like [INAUDIBLE]

00:48:47.894 --> 00:48:53.150
like [INAUDIBLE], it's difficult to
comment on the variation at each stage.

00:48:53.150 --> 00:48:56.430
But what is important is that they
do bring value to the companies,

00:48:56.430 --> 00:48:59.560
to the whole economy.

00:48:59.560 --> 00:49:02.689
What I would be very worried about, is,

00:49:02.689 --> 00:49:06.940
when those companies
are [INAUDIBLE] a very thin value.

00:49:06.940 --> 00:49:13.070
I want to raise, use one examples.

00:49:13.070 --> 00:49:19.780
Back in 2001 and 2002, there's a bunch
of companies called SP company in China.

00:49:19.780 --> 00:49:22.610
I don't know if you guys still remember.

00:49:22.610 --> 00:49:26.270
Maybe some of you are not
living in China at the time.

00:49:26.270 --> 00:49:29.030
They are providing either linked homes or

00:49:29.030 --> 00:49:34.350
pictures for the China Mobile,
China Unicom etc.

00:49:34.350 --> 00:49:38.160
And most of the companies have died or
have been sold.

00:49:38.160 --> 00:49:42.922
And sold itself is not issue,
issue is when the company is sold it,

00:49:42.922 --> 00:49:45.770
the company almost disappear.

00:49:45.770 --> 00:49:47.760
It's not like a YouTube or Instagram.

00:49:47.760 --> 00:49:50.750
They're there in
the proper valid to users.

00:49:50.750 --> 00:49:52.690
Why does a company disappear?

00:49:52.690 --> 00:49:55.540
At one point, they are very,
very profitable.

00:49:55.540 --> 00:50:03.910
Because they charge you

00:50:03.910 --> 00:50:06.540
the fees through China Mobile and
China Unicom.

00:50:06.540 --> 00:50:11.610
And it can be very profitable,
because the value created is just so thin.

00:50:11.610 --> 00:50:13.580
People do not need that middle man.

00:50:13.580 --> 00:50:16.940
China Mobile can do it
themselves after they fill out.

00:50:16.940 --> 00:50:20.020
So, that's why it was [INAUDIBLE],
that's the real bubble.

00:50:20.020 --> 00:50:24.810
If you talk about the bubble in
the last ten, 15, years in China.

00:50:24.810 --> 00:50:29.720
Those SP company as a whole is a bubble,
and if we can avoid

00:50:29.720 --> 00:50:33.850
that I think they know that almost
startup environment is really healthy.

00:50:35.520 --> 00:50:39.190
&gt;&gt; Great, well, before we end Neil,
one last question.

00:50:39.190 --> 00:50:41.570
So as a really successful
entrepreneur yourself,

00:50:41.570 --> 00:50:44.990
and now being the entrepreneur
behind entrepreneurs.

00:50:44.990 --> 00:50:49.010
What advice would you have for aspiring
entrepreneurs here in the audience

00:50:49.010 --> 00:50:50.350
as they look to build something new?

00:50:51.610 --> 00:50:54.252
&gt;&gt; I think you have to go with your heart.

00:50:54.252 --> 00:50:58.240
[LAUGH] It's difficult to
make a perfect choice.

00:50:59.530 --> 00:51:05.550
And, at least in my case, is that
each time when I make a choice I say,

00:51:05.550 --> 00:51:08.280
one, whether this go with a big trend.

00:51:08.280 --> 00:51:15.640
Secondly, and probably more important,
which is something you really like to do?

00:51:15.640 --> 00:51:23.760
When I was working at Deutsche Bank, and
in the last sort of a year or so in 1999.

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:30.600
I really, really was excited by
the Internet development in China.

00:51:30.600 --> 00:51:31.990
I really want to do something.

00:51:31.990 --> 00:51:36.242
If I'm not going to do Sea Trip,
I would do something else.

00:51:36.242 --> 00:51:39.630
But Sea Trip itself fits me
well because I love travelling.

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:45.530
At the same time, I think the Internet
can change people's life in China.

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:48.320
So you have to really love what you do,

00:51:48.320 --> 00:51:51.490
otherwise it's going to
be difficult to sustain.

00:51:51.490 --> 00:51:54.790
If you just think this
is just a big trend, but

00:51:54.790 --> 00:51:59.390
there's something which you don't like or
you don't know whether you like it or not.

00:51:59.390 --> 00:52:02.970
Then if you work there for
like 6 months and 12 months,

00:52:02.970 --> 00:52:06.350
you'll probably feel like, oh,
I want to jump to the next trend.

00:52:06.350 --> 00:52:09.920
I do notice there's some
entrepreneur like that.

00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:14.600
We have this Monday meeting and
then when some entrepreneur

00:52:14.600 --> 00:52:19.200
come to visit and ask him, so
why you want to do this entrepreneurship?

00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:23.190
Oh, B to B is hot or big data is hot.

00:52:23.190 --> 00:52:27.960
But the question fundamental is, do you
think you are the best fit for this?

00:52:27.960 --> 00:52:30.480
And do you really have passion for that?

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:33.708
And that's a question
you should ask yourself.

00:52:33.708 --> 00:52:37.520
No matter what other people's interest,
you should find your interest and

00:52:37.520 --> 00:52:41.560
follow your heart, and find the stuff
you are passionate about it and do it.

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:44.610
&gt;&gt; All right, thank you so much, Neil.

00:52:44.610 --> 00:52:46.082
Real pleasure having you here today.

00:52:46.082 --> 00:52:47.184
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:52:47.184 --> 00:52:51.450
[MUSIC]

